For arbitrary $k$, define $\mathcal P_k$ to have $k$-modules as objects and \[ \mathcal P_k(X,Y)= igoplus_{d\geq0} \operatorname{Hom}_k(\Gamma^d X,Y) \] as morphisms. These are the polynomial laws with a globally finite degree bound. Composition is composition of laws after every scalar extension. This is a strict polynomial category in the representability sense of cote~137, scans~75--84. Its additive degree-one subcategory is $\operatorname{Mod}_k$, its finite products are direct sums, and its representing tensor product and divided powers are the usual ones. For positive integers or zero $p,q$, let \[ \alpha_{q,p}^X:\Gamma^{pq}X\longrightarrow \Gamma^q(\Gamma^pX) \] be the linear map representing the degree-$pq$ law $x\mapsto\gamma_q(\gamma_p(x))$. For $p=0$ this uses the law with constant value $1\in\Gamma^0X=k$, so $\alpha_{q,0}(1)=\gamma_q(1)$. For homogeneous $f:\Gamma^pX	o Y$ and $g:\Gamma^qY	o Z$, composition is exactly \[ g\mathbin{\star}f=g\,\Gamma^q(f)\,\alpha_{q,p}^X: \Gamma^{pq}X\longrightarrow Z. 	ag{A1.3} \] For an inhomogeneous $f=\sum_{i=0}^m f_i$ and homogeneous $g_q$, its component of degree $n$ is \[ \sum_{\substack{
u_0+\cdots+
u_m=q\ \sum i
u_i=n}} g_q\,\mu_
u^Y \left( igotimes_{i=0}^m\Gamma^{
u_i}(f_i)ight) \left( igotimes_{i=0}^m\alpha_{
u_i,i}^Xight) \Delta_{(i
u_i)_i}^X . 	ag{A1.4} \] In this formula $\Delta$ is the component of the divided-power comultiplication $\Gamma^nX	o igotimes_i\Gamma^{i
u_i}X$, and $\mu_
u^Y: igotimes_i\Gamma^{
u_i}Y	o\Gamma^qY$ is multiplication in $\Gamma^*Y$. In particular every displayed arrow is linear in $\operatorname{Mod}_k$. The sum is finite, even when constants occur.
